    "text": "Homeostasis is the regulation of the body’s internal conditions. Many internal conditions are controlled by hormones. Homeostasis works by negative feedback control. ADH is a hormone made by the pituitary gland. ADH controls how much water is reabsorbed from the kidney tubules. Table 2 shows effects of ADH. Table 2 The concentration of ADH in a man’s blood was 3.75 nanograms/dm3. The concentration of ADH in his blood decreased to 1.25 nanograms/dm3. Explain how the decrease in the concentration of ADH would cause the changes to the urine shown in Table 2.",
    "answer": "(kidney) tubules less permeable to water (so) less water is reabsorbed (so) more water in urine causing increased rate of production (of urine) (so) more water in urine causing lower concentration of dissolved substances (in urine)\n(examiner note: allow (so) less water taken back into the blood allow (so) more water in urine causing increased volume (of urine) (so) more water in urine causing more dilute urine)",
    "source": "AQA GCSE Biology Paper 2H June 2023 Q05.3",
